Observed failure: In journey step 0, seeking element "#nav-apply". After pressing Tab? Actually test expects after seek action, assertion ax name "Apply" role "link" passes but fails because observed=0 meaning accessibility tree didn't expose the link as a link? Likely due to missing accessible name? But the link has inner text "Apply". However maybe the issue is that the navigation list is hidden via aria-hidden="true"? Let's examine source: In index.html, nav ul has aria-label="Navigation menu" but no aria-hidden attribute. Wait we saw earlier in observed before snippet: `<ul id=\"navigation\" class=\"govuk-header__navigation\" aria-label=\"Navigation menu\" aria-hidden=\"true\">` That appears in observed before body HTML. Indeed the observed before shows aria-hidden="true". But source index.html does NOT have aria-hidden attribute. So where does aria-hidden come from? Possibly added by JS when menu closed? The header component likely adds aria-hidden based on menu state. Since menu is closed (aria-expanded false), they set aria-hidden true on the menu. That hides the navigation from assistive tech, making links not exposed as links. For keyboard users, they can still tab to them? Actually if aria-hidden true, elements are removed from accessibility tree but still focusable? Usually aria-hidden removes from accessibility tree but does not prevent focus. However the test expects role link and name Apply. It fails because accessible name not exposed? Possibly because the link is inside an element with aria-hidden true, causing it to be ignored.

Thus fix: Ensure that when menu is closed (mobile view?), the navigation should not be hidden from assistive tech on desktop? Actually the header component likely intended for responsive: on small screens, menu button toggles navigation visibility; when hidden, should be aria-hidden true. On desktop, navigation is always visible, so should not have aria-hidden. The bug might be that the component incorrectly sets aria-hidden regardless of viewport. The observed state shows menu-button aria-expanded false (closed) and navigation aria-hidden true, even though it's desktop width (since nav items visible). The CSS shows .govuk-header__navigation displayed as inline-block (desktop). The JS should not hide navigation when menu is closed on desktop; only when menu button is not displayed (i.e., when viewport small). The component likely should check if menu button is displayed (i.e., not display:none) before applying aria-hidden.

In header.js, syncState sets aria-hidden on menu based on isVisible (which is whether menu has class govuk-header__navigation--open). This class is toggled by menu button click. Initially, menu does not have that class, so isVisible false, thus aria-hidden true. That's wrong for desktop where menu should be visible without needing open class. The CSS shows that .govuk-header__navigation is displayed by default (inline-block). The open class likely only for mobile to show when menu button clicked. So we need to adjust syncState: only set aria-hidden when menu button is not displayed (i.e., when menu button's computed style display != none) OR when we are in mobile mode. Simpler: we can remove aria-hidden attribute altogether and rely on CSS to hide/show. But spec says when hidden, should be aria-hidden true. So we need to conditionally set aria-hidden based on whether menu is visually hidden (i.e., when menu button is displayed and menu not open). Let's implement: compute if menuButton.offsetParent !== null (i.e., not display none). If menuButton is visible (mobile), then menu should be hidden unless open. Else (desktop), menu should be visible (aria-hidden false).

